// RELIGIONS AND PHILOSOPHY OF INDIA — English version (same structure and image ids as dados.js).
// Chronology is fragile: most dates of texts and teachers are estimates and many are debated (flagged in the text). BC = before Christ, AD = anno Domini.

const visao = [
  { caixa: 'In brief', texto: [
    'India gave birth to, or gave their familiar shape to, some of humanity’s great religious and philosophical traditions: the **Vedic religion** and its sacred texts, the **Upanishads**, **Buddhism**, **Jainism**, the philosophical schools called **darshanas**, the epics **Mahabharata** and **Ramayana** with the **Bhagavad Gita**, the worship of **Shiva**, **Vishnu** and the **Goddess**, and the great movements of devotion (*bhakti*). Later, from the 8th century, **Islam** arrived and became an integral part of the subcontinent’s religious landscape.',
    'This page follows that story from the first Vedic hymns (c. 1500 BC, dates debated) to c. AD 1500, when the Portuguese reach Calicut. It is a history of **debate** more than of dogma: Brahmins, Buddhist monks, Jain ascetics and materialist philosophers argued in public, for centuries, about what the world, the self and liberation are. The people who lived in that world did not see separate “religions” as we do today, but paths, schools, sects and lineages of teachers.'
  ] },
  { img: 'irf-varanasi-ghats', leg: 'The ghats of Varanasi on the Ganges: the city is one of India’s oldest holy places and still a major centre of pilgrimage.' },
  { h: 'What we mean by “the religions of India”' },
  'The word “religion” is European and relatively recent. Sanskrit has no exact equivalent; it speaks of **dharma** (order, duty, law, teaching), of **marga** (path) and of **darshana** (“viewpoint”, a philosophical school). The word “**Hinduism**” itself was coined by European authors (and then adopted by Indians) only in the early 19th century; “Hindu” comes from Persian and at first designated the people of the river Indus (*Sindhu*). Historians therefore speak of Hinduism as a **set of traditions** with texts, gods and practices shared to differing degrees, not as a church with a single creed.',
  'Buddhism and Jainism, by contrast, have identifiable founders and well-defined monastic communities from early on. Islam, Christianity, Judaism and Zoroastrianism came to India from outside, at different times, and became Indian.',
  { h: 'Four ideas that run through almost everything' },
  { lista: [
    '**Dharma:** the order of the world and each person’s duty within it; in Buddhism, also the Buddha’s teaching.',
    '**Karma:** the principle that actions (and intentions) have consequences that shape this life and the next.',
    '**Samsara:** the cycle of births and deaths in which beings are trapped.',
    '**Moksha / nirvana:** liberation from that cycle. It is the goal of almost every tradition, though each describes the path differently (knowledge, ritual, devotion, asceticism, mental discipline).'
  ] },
  'These ideas do not appear in the oldest hymns of the Rigveda; they take shape around the 1st millennium BC and are discussed, accepted or rejected in very different ways by the different schools. The materialists of the Charvaka, for example, rejected them.',
  { img: 'irf-sacrificio-vedico', leg: 'Artist’s reconstruction: a Vedic sacrifice, with priests pouring ghee into the fire, c. 1000 BC.' },
  { h: 'When and where' },
  'The chronology is difficult, because the oldest texts were transmitted orally for centuries and only much later written down (the surviving manuscripts are mostly medieval or modern). The main phases:',
  { tabela: { cab: ['Phase', 'Approximate dates', 'What marks it'], linhas: [
    ['Vedic religion', 'c. 1500 – 600 BC', 'Hymns of the Rigveda; the other three Vedas; Brahmanas; fire sacrifice, in the north-west and then on the Ganges'],
    ['Upanishads and *shramana* movements', 'c. 800 – 300 BC', 'First Upanishads; wandering ascetics; Buddha, Mahavira, the Ajivikas; *karma*, *samsara* and *moksha* emerge as central problems'],
    ['Organised Buddhism and Jainism', 'c. 300 BC – c. AD 500', 'Ashoka and the spread of Buddhism; councils; Mahayana; art of Gandhara and Mathura; Jainism in the kingdoms of the north and the Deccan'],
    ['Temple Hinduism, epics and darshanas', 'c. 200 BC – c. AD 700', 'Mahabharata and Ramayana take shape; Bhagavad Gita; Manusmriti; worship of Vishnu, Shiva and Devi; first stone temples; texts of the six schools'],
    ['Vedanta, bhakti and great temples', 'c. AD 600 – 1500', 'Alvars and Nayanars; Shankara, Ramanuja, Madhva; Chola temples; Kabir and other poet-saints'],
    ['Islam in the subcontinent', 'c. AD 700 – 1500', 'Sindh (711 – 712); Mahmud of Ghazni; Al-Biruni; Delhi Sultanate (1206); Sufis; encounters and conflicts with local traditions']
  ] } },
  { img: 'irf-mapa-budismo', leg: 'Map of the spread of Buddhism from India to Sri Lanka, Central Asia, China and South-East Asia.' },
  'The setting is the whole Indian subcontinent, from the Indus to the Ganges and from the Himalaya to Kerala and Sri Lanka, plus the places these ideas reached (Central Asia, China, South-East Asia). The centre of early Buddhism and Jainism was the **middle Ganges** (Magadha); that of temple Hinduism ended up being the **Deccan and the south**.',
  { h: 'Two great currents: Brahmins and shramanas' },
  'Historians usually distinguish two great currents. The first is the **Brahmanical** (or Vedic), centred on the Vedas, on sacrifice and on the authority of the priests, the **Brahmins**. The second is that of the **shramanas**, wandering ascetics who left home, begged for food and sought liberation through meditation and discipline, without accepting the authority of the Vedas: the Buddhists, the Jains, the Ajivikas and others. There was conflict and rivalry, but also constant mutual influence: the idea of renunciation, non-violence (*ahimsa*) and the growing vegetarianism of Hinduism owe much to dialogue with the shramanas.',
  { h: 'Why they matter' },
  { lista: [
    '**Half the world inherited them:** Buddhism is today a world religion, from Sri Lanka to Japan; Hinduism is the religion of about 80% of India’s inhabitants (2011 census: 79.8%).',
    '**Philosophy without a rigid divide between faith and reason:** the Indian schools worked out logic, theory of knowledge, ontology, philosophy of language and analysis of the mind, with public debates between rivals.',
    '**Ethics and politics:** non-violence (*ahimsa*) and Ashoka’s tolerance, the idea of duty (*dharma*), the discussion of just war in the Gita.',
    '**Literature:** the two great epics, the Puranas, devotional poetry in Sanskrit, Tamil, Kannada, Hindi and Bengali.',
    '**Art and architecture:** the stupa, the image of the Buddha and the image of Shiva or Vishnu, the stone temple, the Chola bronze.'
  ] },
  { img: 'irf-rigveda-manuscrito', leg: 'Manuscript of the Rigveda in Devanagari script (a late copy: the hymns were transmitted orally for many centuries).' },
  { h: 'A caution about words' },
  'Three precautions help in reading this history. **First**, Indian religious traditions tell their origins in the language of tradition and faith; this page distinguishes, wherever possible, **tradition** from **documented history**. **Second**, much of what we know about rivals (Ajivikas, Charvakas) reached us only through their opponents. **Third**, terms such as “caste”, “Hinduism” or “yoga” now have modern meanings, often different from the ancient ones (postural yoga, for example, is largely a modern practice).',
  { caixa: 'The religions of India today', texto: 'According to India’s 2011 census, the population was about **79.8% Hindu**, 14.2% Muslim, 2.3% Christian, 1.7% Sikh, 0.7% Buddhist and 0.4% Jain. Buddhism, which was born in India, almost disappeared from the country between the 12th and 19th centuries and was revived in the 20th (Bodh Gaya, Ambedkar’s movement from 1956); Jainism, though small, has enormous social influence, especially in Gujarat, Rajasthan and Karnataka. Followers of Hinduism and Buddhism are now found all over the world.' },
  { img: 'irf-nataraja-chola', leg: 'Shiva as Nataraja, “lord of the dance”, Chola-period bronze (southern India, 11th – 12th centuries).' }
];

const linha = [
  'This timeline follows the development of ideas and communities. The dates are approximate; almost all those before Ashoka (3rd century BC) are estimates, and several are debated among historians.',
  { linha: [
    { d: 'c. 2600 – 1900 BC', t: 'The Indus valley: signs of worship', x: 'The cities of the Indus civilisation (Harappa, Mohenjo-daro) left seals, figures and monumental baths that suggest ritual practices, but we **do not know** what gods or beliefs they had, as their script is undeciphered. The famous “Pashupati seal”, with a seated figure with horned headgear, has been read by some as “proto-Shiva”; that is a hypothesis, not a fact.' },
    { d: 'c. 1500 – 1000 BC (debated)', t: 'The hymns of the Rigveda', x: 'In north-western India the 1028 hymns of the **Rigveda** (about 10,600 verses) are composed in Vedic Sanskrit, addressed to gods such as **Indra**, **Agni**, **Soma**, **Varuna** and **Ushas**. They were transmitted orally, with extraordinary memorisation techniques, for centuries.' },
    { d: 'c. 1000 – 600 BC', t: 'The other Vedas and the Brahmanas', x: 'The *Samaveda* (chants), the *Yajurveda* (ritual formulas) and the *Atharvaveda* (spells and charms) take shape, followed by the **Brahmanas**, treatises on sacrifice. The solemn ritual (*shrauta*) grows in complexity and the power of the Brahmins increases.' },
    { d: 'c. 800 – 500 BC', t: 'The first Upanishads', x: 'The *Brihadaranyaka* and the *Chandogya* (the oldest) question the meaning of ritual and propose that what matters is knowledge of the identity between the self (*atman*) and the foundation of the universe (*brahman*). The ideas of *samsara* and *moksha* appear there.' },
    { d: '6th – 5th c. BC (debated)', t: 'The shramana movements: Buddha, Mahavira, Ajivikas', x: 'In the middle Ganges valley, ascetics and teachers compete. **Siddhartha Gautama (the Buddha)** and **Vardhamana Mahavira** found communities of monks and nuns; **Makkhali Gosala** leads the Ajivikas. The dates of the Buddha (traditionally c. 563 – 483 BC; many historians place his death between c. 410 and 400 BC) and of Mahavira are debated.' },
    { d: 'c. 5th – 4th c. BC', t: 'First Buddhist councils', x: 'According to tradition, just after the Buddha’s death the **First Council** met at Rajagriha, where monks recited his teachings; a century later the Second, at Vaishali, dealt with disputes over monastic discipline. The details come from late sources and are discussed.' },
    { d: 'c. 268 – 232 BC', t: 'Ashoka and the *dhamma*', x: 'After the war of Kalinga, the emperor **Ashoka** promotes a *dhamma* of social morality and tolerance (Edict 12: respect the sects of others). He supports Buddhists, Brahmins, Jains and **Ajivikas** (the Barabar caves are donated to the latter). Buddhist tradition speaks of a Third Council at Pataliputra and of the sending of the monk **Mahinda** to Sri Lanka.' },
    { d: 'c. 300 BC – AD 300', t: 'The epics take shape', x: 'The **Mahabharata** and the **Ramayana** take shape gradually from the stories of bards, and reach the version we know only in the first centuries AD. They become the great repository of narratives, ethics and popular theology.' },
    { d: 'c. 200 BC – AD 200', t: 'The Bhagavad Gita and the law texts', x: 'The **Bhagavad Gita**, inserted in the Mahabharata, and the treatises on *dharma* (the **Dharmashastras**, among them the **Manusmriti**, c. 200 BC – AD 200) take shape. The Gita joins action, knowledge and devotion in a teaching to Arjuna on duty.' },
    { d: 'c. 113 BC', t: 'The Heliodorus pillar', x: 'At Besnagar (near Vidisha), **Heliodorus**, Greek ambassador of the Indo-Greek king Antialcidas, erects a pillar to **Vasudeva** (Krishna) and describes himself as a *bhagavata*, a devotee: one of the oldest testimonies of Vaishnava worship and proof of how a Greek adopted an Indian devotion.' },
    { d: '1st c. BC', t: 'The Pali canon is written down', x: 'In Sri Lanka, in the reign of Vattagamani (c. 29 – 17 BC according to Geiger’s chronology; the traditional chronology places him c. 89 – 77 BC), monks commit the texts of the **Tipitaka** to writing in Pali. Until then they had been kept in memory; this is the basis of Theravada Buddhism.' },
    { d: '1st – 2nd c. AD', t: 'Mahayana and the images of the Buddha', x: 'The first works of **Mahayana** (“Great Vehicle”) appear, with the bodhisattva ideal, as do the first images of the Buddha at **Gandhara** and **Mathura**, in the time of the Kushanas. Before that, the Buddha was represented by symbols (wheel, tree, footprints).' },
    { d: 'c. AD 150 – 250', t: 'Nagarjuna', x: 'The Buddhist philosopher **Nagarjuna** founds the **Madhyamaka** school, the “middle way”, with the concept of *shunyata* (“emptiness”). He is one of the most influential thinkers of Asia.' },
    { d: '2nd c. BC – 5th c. AD', t: 'The texts of the six schools', x: 'The foundations of the **darshanas** are written, in aphorisms (*sutras*) with commentaries: Nyaya, Vaisheshika, Samkhya, Yoga, Mimamsa and Vedanta. Fixing dates is hard; many texts were expanded over centuries.' },
    { d: '4th – 6th c. AD', t: 'The Guptas and temple Hinduism', x: 'Under the **Guptas** and their contemporaries the cults of **Vishnu**, **Shiva** and the **Goddess** consolidate; the first stone temples (Deogarh), the **Puranas** and the *Devi Mahatmya* appear. Buddhism and Jainism continue to flourish (Nalanda, the caves of Ajanta).' },
    { d: '4th – 5th c. AD', t: 'Vasubandhu and Asanga; Buddhist logic', x: 'The brothers **Asanga** and **Vasubandhu** develop the **Yogachara** school (the mind as the centre of experience). Later, **Dignaga** (c. 480 – 540) and **Dharmakirti** (7th c.) create Buddhist logic and theory of knowledge.' },
    { d: 'c. AD 454 – 466 (tradition)', t: 'The Jain canon is fixed', x: 'According to tradition, at Valabhi (Gujarat) a council led by **Devardhigani** commits to writing the texts (*Agamas*) of the Svetambara line. The Digambaras reject these texts as incomplete.' },
    { d: 'c. 629 – 645', t: 'Xuanzang in India', x: 'The Chinese monk **Xuanzang** (602 – 664) travels through India, studies at Nalanda and returns with hundreds of manuscripts. His account describes Buddhist monasteries, Hindu and Jain temples, and the rivalry between them.' },
    { d: '6th – 9th c. AD', t: 'Bhakti in Tamil Nadu', x: 'The **Nayanars** (poet-saints of Shiva) and the **Alvars** (of Vishnu) sing in Tamil of personal and emotional devotion, in temples and on pilgrimages. Among the Alvars is **Andal**, one of the rare female voices.' },
    { d: '711 – 712', t: 'The Arabs in Sindh', x: 'The Umayyad general **Muhammad ibn al-Qasim** conquers Sindh from King Dahir. It is the first stable Islamic presence in the subcontinent (trade relations with Arab merchants, on the Malabar and Gujarat coasts, are older).' },
    { d: '8th c. (debated)', t: 'Shankara', x: 'The philosopher **Adi Shankara** systematises **Advaita Vedanta** (non-dualism) in commentaries on the Upanishads, the Gita and the Brahma Sutras. Historians place him c. 700 – 750 (tradition gives 788 – 820; and some traditions, much earlier). The *mathas* (monasteries) attributed to him are perhaps a later organisation.' },
    { d: '9th – 12th c.', t: 'The peak of the southern temples and of Shaivism', x: 'The **Cholas** build Thanjavur (1010) and Gangaikondacholapuram, and the Rashtrakutas had carved, in the 8th century, the Kailasa at Ellora. In Kashmir, **Abhinavagupta** (c. 950 – 1016) writes on aesthetics and on non-dual Shaivism.' },
    { d: 'c. 1030', t: 'Al-Biruni and the *Kitab al-Hind*', x: 'The scholar **Al-Biruni**, who accompanied the campaigns of Mahmud of Ghazni, writes a unique work on India: religion, philosophy, science, customs. It is one of the great medieval examples of respectful study of a foreign civilisation.' },
    { d: '11th – 12th c.', t: 'Ramanuja and the southern Vaishnavas', x: '**Ramanuja** (tradition gives 1017 – 1137) defends **Vishishtadvaita** (“qualified non-dualism”): God is personal and souls are his real parts. He gives theological foundation to devotion to Vishnu. **Basava**, in Karnataka (12th c.), founds the **Lingayat** movement, critical of Brahmin ritual and hierarchies.' },
    { d: 'c. 1193 – 1206', t: 'The Delhi Sultanate', x: 'The armies of Muhammad of Ghor defeat the Rajputs (Tarain, 1192) and **Qutb ud-Din Aibak** founds the **Delhi Sultanate** in 1206. Buddhist monasteries of Bihar (Nalanda, Vikramashila) are destroyed around 1200 and Buddhism declines rapidly in the north; it had already been losing royal and lay support. Sufis arrive in the north, among them **Moinuddin Chishti** (d. 1236, Ajmer).' },
    { d: '13th – 15th c.', t: 'Poet-saints and new communities', x: '**Jnaneshwar** (13th c., Maharashtra), **Ramananda**, **Kabir** (15th c.) and **Guru Nanak** (1469 – 1539) preach a direct devotion, in the languages of the people, sometimes among Hindus and Muslims alike. From Nanak’s teaching **Sikhism** is born. In 1336 Vijayanagara is founded, protecting southern Hinduism and the philosophy of **Vidyaranya**; **Madhva** (13th c.) had founded **Dvaita**.' },
    { d: '1498', t: 'Vasco da Gama at Calicut', x: 'The Portuguese fleet reaches **Calicut** (May 1498), the kingdom of the Zamorin, where Hindus, Muslims (the **Mappila** merchants and others), Jews and Christians live side by side. According to the voyage’s *Roteiro*, some Portuguese entered a Hindu temple thinking it was a Christian church. A new stage in India’s relations with Europe begins, outside the scope of this page.' }
  ] }
];

